1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:10,240
Hey, you're listening to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing subscription app businesses.

2
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:14,560
We'll share insider secrets from the top subscription apps on the app stores.

3
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:15,840
Let's get into the show.

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:22,800
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard, and with me today, RevenueCat CEO, Jacob Heiding.

5
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:30,000
Our guest today is Curtis Herbert, an independent iOS app developer, designer and wearer of many hats.

6
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:54,720
Curtis is the founder of Slopes, the app for skiing and snowboarding, and he took it from an indie side project to a thriving On the podcast, we talk with Curtis about his nine year journey to reach a million dollars in ARR, why he shares revenue numbers publicly and how taking inspiration from web businesses instead of other apps kept him ahead of the curve.

7
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:58,640
Hey Curtis, I'm so excited to have you on the podcast today.

8
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:02,480
Long time fan, first time podcasting with you.

9
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:08,400
So I wanted to jump right in and just ask, like, why did you build a snowboarding app?

10
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:23,760
Because I snowboard is probablying I was with some friends and we were using another app at the time that was pretty well engineered but the UX left a little bit to be desired to be polite about it.

11
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:30,400
And so we were trying to figure out I think where we went the fastest while we were skiing that day.

12
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:36,160
And always remember people ski responsibly, don't go out of control fast, go at a controlled speed.

13
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:44,000
But anyway, we wanted to see where we went the fastest and it took cross referencing like three or four screens or something crazy.

14
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:51,760
So I was just we were at Denny's and I'm like, yo, I could do better than this, which I feel like is the famous last words for software.

15
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:55,440
engineer and the rest is nine years of pain and suffering.

16
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,080
So you started with the very typical, like build a better mouse trap.

17
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:03,760
Yes, yes.

18
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:04,800
That's awesome.

19
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:19,840
Advice I actually give people to not do, but maybe a combination of being early Sometimes the mouse traps are really bad, David, and like somebody, you know, and it just takes the person that is annoyed enough to fix it, right?

20
00:02:20,960 --> 00:02:23,200
So I mean, it's classic.

21
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:26,240
Like you can't solve a problem you don't have personally, I kind of feel like.

22
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:28,240
And so that's a great place to start.

23
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:32,000
Yeah, and I certainly wasn't the first on the market at that time.

24
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:34,000
There were like one or two or three other apps.

25
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:37,120
So yeah, it was a space that had competition already.

26
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:40,480
So, you know, take that as you will.

27
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:42,560
It's a good indication that there's a market there.

28
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:47,920
But then, you know, competition and I had to come from behind to compete with that.

29
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:48,560
Yeah.

30
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:51,440
So how long did it take you to get to 1.0?

31
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:54,480
Was this just it was a you were working full time at the time, right?

32
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:56,400
So this was just started as a side project?

33
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:03,520
Yeah, I had left Lockheed Martin in 2011 and I had started my own consulting company for iOS and web.

34
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:16,320
So at the time that I started Slopes, I think the first year I spent only one month full time on it, and then the rest was nights and weekends, and then the other eleven months was consulting.

35
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:29,040
And then year over year, basically as it grew, I was able to use the revenue that it brought in to justify doing less consulting and just kind of balance act that until there was no consulting left in 2016 when I went full time on it.

36
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:29,840
That's awesome.

37
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:31,760
So it shipped in 2013?

38
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:32,640
Yeah.

39
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:41,120
I think I, yeah, I submitted for the iOS 7 launch because that was a fun one to redesign for when you're trying to launch an app.

40
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:45,120
And actually I found a last minute bug and had to reject and resubmit.

41
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:48,000
So I missed the launch day by like three days or something.

42
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:50,960
But whenever iOS seven came out, that's when Slopes came out.

43
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:55,520
Basically, it's interesting to think like iOS seven felt like so late already.

44
00:03:55,840 --> 00:04:00,720
You know, if you think like if you've been doing iOS since 2008 or whatever, it's like, ah, we've gone through seven iterations.

45
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:01,680
Oh yeah.

46
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:02,160
Whatever.

47
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:10,720
But if you think about like where the devices were in that era, like 2012, 2013, like there were apps, but like batteries were really kind of crummy.

48
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:12,320
You couldn't run location all the time.

49
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:14,640
Location APIs were probably much less efficient.

50
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:19,280
Like, you know, there was there's and I think this is generally true when you're trying to.

51
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:21,280
move into a crowded market.

52
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:29,600
If the technology, if the something has changed about the underlying technology, and in this case, maybe it wasn't so obvious, it was a bunch of different things, like that's often a good time to disrupt, right?

53
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:35,200
So we were seven iterations of iOS in, but like maybe we were ready for like a new snowboarding app, right?

54
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:37,360
But it may not have been obvious.

55
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:45,920
Yeah, it was the one of the catalysts for it too, beyond trying to figure out speed and stuff was I wanted to visualize the runs in 3Ds.

56
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:55,520
And phones had kind of caught up to the point where I could write very primitive, teach myself OpenGL kind of code to pre-secure.

57
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:55,920
Essentially.

58
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:57,040
Before Seekit?

59
00:04:57,440 --> 00:04:58,960
Oh, this was well before Seekit.

60
00:04:59,040 --> 00:04:59,120
Yeah.

61
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:05,760
This was running on like an iPhone 4, like a little 3D graph essentially of each run.

62
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:10,400
Now eventually that baby kind of grew up and got rewritten.

63
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:18,960
So I guess I kind of had a new baby into Seekit and then I was actually doing 3D mountains where I would render your run on that mountain itself.

64
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:30,880
But yeah, fortunately I wasn't coming out on the original Edge phone or something and trying to do these fancy Open 3D or OpenGL 3D graphs because that probably wouldn't have done too well.

65
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:35,040
I was just pushing the edge on like three GSs and four s.

66
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:36,160
Wow.

67
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:45,360
And so then, so you launched in 2013 and I even forget, did you launch as a as a paid app or a free within app purchase?

68
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:45,920
No.

69
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:54,160
So I actually went against the grain at the time because this was race to the bottom era and I went premium paid upfront 499, a whole five bucks.

70
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:55,200
Oh, wow.

71
00:05:56,080 --> 00:05:56,160
Yeah.

72
00:05:56,720 --> 00:06:07,360
And that was the launch eventually, I think a year later or so, I started playing with the price and up it to 799 is where it ended up until I went subscription and free.

73
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:12,880
But yeah, I never did the in-app purchase 99 cents, you know, make it up in volume kinda thing.

74
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:14,160
And I'm really glad I did.

75
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:26,560
So, and then, I mean, you've been super open and we're going to talk more about this later, but you know, you've been super open about your, your numbers and share, you know, regularly about your, your revenue and all that.

76
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:31,760
So I'm just curious, what, how much did you make with it paid up front?

77
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:32,960
Did you have a big launch?

78
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:33,920
Did you get a little press?

79
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:35,840
I mean, that was kind of the deal back then, right?

80
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:42,080
It was like, you know, when my Launch Inner Pro app launched, it was like 299, but we made like 60 K in a week.

81
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:47,600
Um, so did you have that kind of a big launch and get some press or or did it just kind of trickle in?.

82
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:48,640
Oh, God, no.

83
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:51,760
It was pretty bad.

84
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:55,440
Um, because I mean, I was coming out of nowhere to do this.

85
00:06:55,840 --> 00:06:57,840
Like, I didn't have an audience really.

86
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:06,720
Um, I'd been in the iOS community a little bit, but yeah, it was no underscore with eighty apps already in the market that I could build on top of that audience or anything like that.

87
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:09,920
So no, the launch I was quite disappointed with.

88
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:25,760
Um, I mean, I went at WWDC, um, some of the Apple press, not Apple, but people who reported on Apple, um, were kind enough to volunteer some of their time at one of the bars and talk to a bunch of indies and people trying to make apps to be like, hey, how can we get your attention?

89
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:27,920
You know, how should we have a press kit stuff like that?

90
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:31,440
And I remember talking to them and then saying, Oh my God, you have an amazing app.

91
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:32,880
Like, yeah, it kind of sucks.

92
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:34,000
Nobody has written about it.

93
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:50,720
And finally, Peter Cohen from IMORE at the time gave me my first press article in the Apple Tech blog ecosystem like a year after it launched or something like that.

94
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:52,880
Just kind of went out on a limb.

95
00:07:53,360 --> 00:08:00,800
Did you have to, did you have to work that a little bit, do PR or was it just you did the you knew him or, or I.

96
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:08,880
I probably almost like No, I was in a stranger world, but No, yeah, I was a total stranger and it was just, uh, you know, hey, I want to help you out.

97
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:10,400
This is good.

98
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:11,440
You should be noticed.

99
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:12,400
Want to help you out.

100
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:17,600
Yeah, the Apple, the people in the Apple press space are, you know, very kind, generous people.

101
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:22,800
Uh, but one of the things I've learned with the ski app is not a lot of Apple people ski.

102
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:31,760
So it's very hard, for example, for Mac Stories to justify covering slopes because ten percent of their audience might ski.

103
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:38,800
I don't know the actual number, but, you know, it's not that relevant versus productivity tools or weather apps or stuff like that.

104
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:56,880
And that was certainly a struggle throughout the history of slopes because most of my connections, interactions, stuff like that come through Twitter and that comes through the iOS community, not necessarily the ski community where it would be better to market this kind of thing.

105
00:08:57,680 --> 00:09:03,280
I think a lot of people sleep on that aspect of PR needing kind of channel fit.

106
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:11,040
It's like you think, oh, if I can just get in TechCrunch or, you know, one of these big publications, it's going to blow things up.

107
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:12,800
But it's exactly what you say.

108
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:23,680
It's like you need to seek out the opportunities in the communities and in the blogs and in the news sites that actually cater to your specific audience.

109
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:28,080
Because like, yeah, so many people who browse TechCrunch, you know, just don't care.

110
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:38,320
And even though the volume is high, the percentage is low and it's just not worth as much as you think it is for certain apps, for certain apps it is, you know, an email or a productivity app probably does a lot better.

111
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:44,800
Um, so anyhow, it sounds like it was a couple of years of really struggling, but you made a little money as a, uh, uh, a side project.

112
00:09:45,280 --> 00:09:46,720
I just looked it up at figures.

113
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:53,600
Um, so I made $10,600 in the first two years of Slopes' existence.ce as a paid up front app.

114
00:09:54,880 --> 00:10:01,280
Not terrible for a sideboard, but not terrible, but considering my consulting rate, it was not paying the bills.

115
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:02,400
I mean, this is Yeah.

116
00:10:02,880 --> 00:10:06,080
This is the thing with building your own equity and IP, right?

117
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:08,640
It's the the outlays are significant, right?

118
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:13,520
Like you have to put you're putting tens or hundreds of thousands of dollars of time into it before you start.

119
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:17,120
I mean, we'll get to the success aspect of this, but it takes a while, right?

120
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:17,840
Yeah.

121
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:21,680
I mean, first season, I think it was 1800 dollars I made.

122
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:26,560
So this is nights and weekends taking a month off work getting a launch, not getting any press.

123
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:29,280
You can imagine it was a little disc disheartening.

124
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:31,280
But, you know, I kept with it.

125
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:34,640
And then the next season got a little bigger and then it just kind of kept going from there.

126
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:35,040
Yeah.

127
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:49,200
So then I did want to talk specifically, you switched to subscriptions in 2015, which is still actually pretty early, which, you know, we we talked to my IQ on on one of the recent episodes and and they were even before that.

128
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:55,360
But 2016 was a year when Apple really kind of opened things up and like any app can do subscriptions.

129
00:10:55,840 --> 00:11:01,200
I'm really curious, like, what way back then inspired you to do subscriptions?

130
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:06,320
And then did you have any like issues with Apple approving a subscription for a snowboarding app app?

131
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:08,560
No issues at all.

132
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:12,560
So what inspired me was the web community.

133
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:15,120
So like I said, I when I was consulting was doing web.

134
00:11:16,080 --> 00:11:18,800
I've been involved with the web since it started.

135
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:22,240
And I've always kept a foot in that arena.

136
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:29,520
So watching Software as a Service take off on the web, you know, everything there was subscription.

137
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:36,720
And I for a long time felt like the web has figured out a lot of stuff that the iOS community is just now figuring out.

138
00:11:37,280 --> 00:11:39,280
They have about a ten year head start on us.

139
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:43,200
So in 2015, I decided, like, hey, I'm going to do this.

140
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:46,000
Like, hey, you know, this app is people love it.

141
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:48,320
You know, the users that I had loved it.

142
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:51,520
But it's not doing what I wanted to do.

143
00:11:51,840 --> 00:11:53,200
I think it could be bigger.

144
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:56,160
The ski market could certainly support a big app, I think.

145
00:11:56,960 --> 00:11:59,840
So I decided to, you know, take a serious run at it basically.

146
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,520
Like, look, what am I going to do to run this like a business, not like a little side app?

147
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:11,680
And at that point I was looking around and there were only a few other apps in the app store that were doing it that weren't dating apps and stuff like that.

148
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:14,400
Like Strava had a subscription, I think.

149
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:22,000
Runkeeper definitely had a subsccriptions.

150
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:26,000
Now, I forget if they were through the App Store or just their web building portals.

151
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:27,520
I don't remember that.

152
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:32,880
But I was looking at them and they were charging 50 bucks a year, stuff like that.

153
00:12:34,080 --> 00:12:37,840
So I kind of felt like, hey, you know, why don't I give that a shot?

154
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:51,680
You know, seeing the web and this is how you succeed, recurring revenue, none of this, you know, free make it up in volume, none of this race to the bottom and compete on price stuff, like charge your value and charge it every year.

155
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:58,080
And I kind of had the epiphany of, hey, wait, people buy season pass.es to go skiing.

156
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:00,800
They're already paying a yearly subscription, basically.

157
00:13:01,920 --> 00:13:05,760
So why can't I just do the same thing and call it a season pass inside the slopes?

158
00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:09,920
And at that point, that was when I really cemented like, I need to try this.

159
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:12,000
Like the ski industry is trying it.

160
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:16,640
There is no reason the app for the ski industry should not also try it.

161
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:21,040
So yeah, when I launched it, it was yearly and monthly.

162
00:13:22,560 --> 00:13:24,080
I got rid of monthly pretty quickly.

163
00:13:24,560 --> 00:13:27,920
But yeah, there were no problems from Apple with the original.

164
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:30,400
So it was non auto renewing back then.

165
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:32,800
Like you pointed out, we hadn't gotten the go ahead yet.

166
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:37,680
And that was just a gambit I was taking that, you know, I would figure out how to self churn.

167
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:41,680
But no problems getting it approved.

168
00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:42,320
It went right through.

169
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:46,480
Yeah, I never got any pushback from that.

170
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:52,400
Some customers were annoyed, but I made sure to take care of the existing paid customers very well.

171
00:13:52,720 --> 00:13:57,920
Still to this day, I'm keeping features unlocked for them, some feature parodies, stuff like that.

172
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:00,160
This is six years, seven years later.

173
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:04,160
And I made sure some of them knew what was coming.

174
00:14:04,400 --> 00:14:06,160
Like this was not a big surprise per se.

175
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:10,960
And made it very clear what they would be getting to be taken care of.

176
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:17,200
And so some of them were annoyed, but I think overall most of them were pretty okay with it.

177
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:20,800
And I saw people to this day write in like, hey, I bought version one.

178
00:14:21,120 --> 00:14:22,560
I'm still rocking the free version.

179
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:24,960
Thanks for keeping it alive kinda thing.

180
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,360
So taking care of them and I didn't have a huge customer base yet.

181
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:33,760
It wasn't, you know, thirty million subscribers or something crazy.

182
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:35,440
Like it was reasonably small.

183
00:14:35,920 --> 00:14:38,480
So the hate email wasn't too overwhelming.

184
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:39,520
Yeah.

185
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:43,600
And if you and if you're doing it right, you most of your customers are in the future, right?

186
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:45,600
It should always kind of be true, you know?

187
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:47,680
So you should take care of.

188
00:14:48,480 --> 00:15:00,240
your existing folks, but like, you know, don't hobble your potential future based on appeasing, especially, you know, if your pricing, I was, I'm curious, like, you went from, uh, you said 799 to a yearly.

189
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:03,120
Do you remember what that price was you anchored on for that first year?

190
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:04,560
Yeah, it was 1999.

191
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:05,360
Uh, okay.

192
00:15:05,680 --> 00:15:11,920
And then I also had 899 monthly, which was designed to make the yearly look better price anchoring, see the web and SAS.

193
00:15:12,720 --> 00:15:20,080
Um, and, uh, I actually got rid of that pretty quickly because I realized a lot of people were buying it and I wasn't sure why because it was a terrible deal.

194
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:24,960
Uh, and then I realized, again, mirroring the ski industry, uh, people want deals.

195
00:15:24,960 --> 00:15:30,800
People want day passes or multiday passes, because a lot of people go skiing for two days a year.

196
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:35,440
And so a season pass wouldn't make sense to them and they were buying the monthly as a way to get around that.

197
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:41,120
So I quickly pivoted, got rid of the monthly and added consumable passes into the mix.

198
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:51,760
Did you, did you, I mean, it seems like the monthly is a good workaround, but I guess from a user experience perspective, you just didn't want people subscribed when they were really trying to just do a daily thing.

199
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:53,120
Yeah.

200
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:56,880
I mean, honestly, it feels like a dark pattern in my industry.

201
00:15:57,040 --> 00:16:10,160
I know one other competitor that is now dead at towards the end tried to trick a lot of people based on some dark UX into monthly and their app reviews were filled with people saying why am I getting billed with this over the summer?

202
00:16:10,320 --> 00:16:12,480
It's a ski app, you know, what's going on?

203
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:20,080
So I figured monthly like it honestly just didn't make sense if I could pull off the consumable thing, which I could.

204
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:31,600
That one at the time, I did get a little pushback on Apple just from the day pass because you couldn't have a essentially what they were considering a subscription be a day long.

205
00:16:32,240 --> 00:16:38,640
So back then, I just had to call it a single use pass, which just happened to activate for 24 hours when you turned it on.

206
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:41,840
But protecting consumers.

207
00:16:42,720 --> 00:16:42,800
Yeah.

208
00:16:43,360 --> 00:16:47,120
It was a minor, like one phone call from App Review and we were clear.

209
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:49,600
And I got it fixed in 24 hours.

210
00:16:49,760 --> 00:16:50,320
Like, it was fine.

211
00:16:51,200 --> 00:16:51,520
That's good.

212
00:16:52,080 --> 00:16:53,920
So what was what, when did you switch?

213
00:16:54,400 --> 00:16:59,120
So are you doing auto renewing now on your on your annuals or did you ever make that switch or is it?

214
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:00,160
Oh, God, yeah.

215
00:17:00,240 --> 00:17:00,960
As soon as it came out.

216
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:02,240
Oh, yeah.

217
00:17:02,880 --> 00:17:12,960
As soon as it came out, I switched to auto renewing, which was, I guess, that happened about two months before my initial set of subscriptions were set to renew.

218
00:17:13,840 --> 00:17:14,880
So the timing was pretty good.

219
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:18,320
It happened., I think, right before the season started, if I remember correctly.

220
00:17:18,560 --> 00:17:18,640
Yeah.

221
00:17:19,360 --> 00:17:26,160
And so then, but you did have to upgrade those initial non renew into the renew, right?

222
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:27,280
So you did have, yeah.

223
00:17:27,440 --> 00:17:30,240
Probably higher churn that year than in future years.

224
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:32,000
Oh, yeah, yeah.

225
00:17:32,640 --> 00:17:33,840
But I mean, that's just part of it.

226
00:17:34,320 --> 00:17:36,720
I mean, there was no clean way to make that transition.

227
00:17:37,040 --> 00:17:37,200
Right.

228
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:44,400
And I was already planning on, I'd have to sell them again on the yearly pass anyway because it wasn't going to be automatic.

229
00:17:44,880 --> 00:17:46,000
So I was prepared for this.

230
00:17:46,320 --> 00:17:47,520
It wasn't a surprise for me.

231
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:52,560
And it was a happy surprise actually that, you know, I'd be transitioning them to an auto renew setup.

232
00:17:53,680 --> 00:18:01,280
And I remember checking the first year with the auto renew that, you know, not too many people were keeping subscribed if they didn't then record the next season.

233
00:18:02,160 --> 00:18:03,520
And that number was very low.

234
00:18:03,840 --> 00:18:08,800
So I was happy that, you know, even though I had switched to auto renew, it wasn't taking advantage of customers.

235
00:18:09,120 --> 00:18:09,200
Yeah.

236
00:18:09,680 --> 00:18:14,400
They had been canceling people I tend to be pretty rational about it, especially for good apps.

237
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:22,240
I think like if you're being very honest and not using dark patterns to trick people into it at the beginning, like folks folks will unsubscribe to these things.

238
00:18:22,480 --> 00:18:27,840
Um, and I actually think it's really, you know, if you if you're building value, people are using it and you're storing logs, right?

239
00:18:28,080 --> 00:18:29,520
Like it's a long term relationship with.

240
00:18:29,920 --> 00:18:30,080
the app.

241
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:33,920
I feel like this is a really great example of an app.

242
00:18:34,160 --> 00:18:35,040
I mean, you knew this.

243
00:18:35,440 --> 00:18:37,200
You knew it for a lot of other people, I think.

244
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:42,800
Yeah, it was something that I definitely had to be prepared for as part of the ski industry.

245
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:44,160
People don't go skiing every year.

246
00:18:44,640 --> 00:18:49,200
You know, you might go on a great ski trip one year and then not go again for two more years, but then you'll come back.

247
00:18:50,320 --> 00:18:57,200
And getting that, getting customers to come back and remember the app has always been a struggle.

248
00:18:58,000 --> 00:18:59,520
Something I still work on to this day.

249
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:03,840
But the fact that they canceled their subscription, like, that's fine.

250
00:19:04,080 --> 00:19:04,800
You're not skiing.

251
00:19:05,120 --> 00:19:05,760
I don't want your money.

252
00:19:06,880 --> 00:19:11,120
I am giving you literally no value at this point, so please stop giving me money.

253
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:22,880
So you launched the app in 2013, you transitioned to subscriptions in 2015, you transitioned to auto renewing subscriptions in 2016.

254
00:19:23,760 --> 00:19:34,240
At what point in that journey, and I imagine subscriptions had something to do with it, did you feel confident enough at the trajectory and the revenue coming in to actually get full time?

255
00:19:35,520 --> 00:19:38,320
So that was 2016, end of it.

256
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:47,760
And I don't think it necessarily had anything to do with the fact that auto renew was happening because I couldn't see what that churn was going to look like until 2017.

257
00:19:48,320 --> 00:19:48,480
Right.

258
00:19:49,200 --> 00:19:53,120
But the revenue that was coming in was starting to look very good.

259
00:19:54,880 --> 00:19:58,480
I think, see, I hate this because now I have to go back and look at the numbers.

260
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:02,160
They're no longer fresh in my head like they used to be because it's so long ago.

261
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:10,480
But I think that year I made like eighty thousand in proceeds and I had more than two X.

262
00:20:10,960 --> 00:20:12,160
I was like two point five X.

263
00:20:12,960 --> 00:20:16,000
The year before that, I think it was like thirty seven thousand or something like that.

264
00:20:16,880 --> 00:20:24,480
So like seeing that multiple and seeing the number that I was at, I was very confident to say, like, hey, I'm going full time at this point.

265
00:20:24,880 --> 00:20:32,720
Worst case, I can back fill some contracts that wouldn't, you know, iOS contracting, especially back then, you know, that a lot of that was high demand.

266
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:40,640
So I wasn't super worried, which was a nice privilege to have to say, like, hey, worst case, this doesn't work out.

267
00:20:40,880 --> 00:20:41,280
I'm fine.

268
00:20:41,840 --> 00:20:45,520
But, you know, it was a bit of a gamble because I was firing a bunch of clients essentially.

269
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
And once you fire them, it's hard to get them back.

270
00:20:49,040 --> 00:20:52,640
So, oh, some risk, but it felt like a very calculated safe risk.

271
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:53,360
Yeah.

272
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:58,800
I mean, I most most folks are they're they're tied to a W2 like a full time job, right?

273
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:00,080
And they're trying to make that decision.

274
00:21:00,240 --> 00:21:00,880
It's harder, right?

275
00:21:01,040 --> 00:21:03,600
I think by chance that you could blend, right?

276
00:21:03,840 --> 00:21:10,960
Like each subsequent year, put a little more time into it and focus and kind of have a soft transition, which, yeah, I mean, I don't know.

277
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:21,040
I don't know what the advice would be for people to architect that, but if you can make it that way, like I think that's you did a really good job of, yeah, not exposing yourself to unnecessary risk, right?

278
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:25,920
Which is, I think, what we're always, you're always concerned about when you're launching something on your own, right?

279
00:21:26,560 --> 00:21:26,640
Yeah.

280
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:33,840
And that the W2 transition happened for me in 2011 when I left my full time job job.

281
00:21:36,480 --> 00:21:43,200
So I had just done that not with an app, but with consulting, but I feel like that's still very similar math and metrics.

282
00:21:43,360 --> 00:21:44,960
And is this smart or is this not?

283
00:21:45,360 --> 00:21:48,720
So yeah, it's just it wasn't with slopes that I had to make that call essentially.

284
00:21:49,600 --> 00:22:03,840
I've given this advice privately a few times and I'll give it publicly on the podcast, but with the job market, what it is, especially for a talented developer in mobile development even more specifically, like there's just so much more demand.

285
00:22:04,880 --> 00:22:10,480
I told a few people that, you know, who were trying to, you know, build their indie apps is negotiate.

286
00:22:10,880 --> 00:22:20,560
Like, I think there's a lot of opportunity these days to do a 30 hour week job, do a 20 hour week job and blend the best of both worlds.

287
00:22:21,440 --> 00:22:33,040
So if you're out there and trying to replicate Curtis' journey and don't have the stomach for consulting, see if you can find another job at 30 hours a week and then have that spare, free time.

288
00:22:33,360 --> 00:22:38,000
I'm sitting here as an employer with many employees that have jobs and I don't know how to, how to weigh in on this one.

289
00:22:38,240 --> 00:22:39,840
So I'll just sit on the sidelines.

290
00:22:40,480 --> 00:22:41,360
That's a very point.

291
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,400
No, you're coming to work tomorrow, David.

292
00:22:45,840 --> 00:22:46,720
This is not allowed.

293
00:22:49,680 --> 00:22:51,920
So I did want to dive into the marketing.

294
00:22:53,600 --> 00:23:01,600
So again, like this, this, this kind of arc that that you've gone on from this, you know, total side project, barely making any money.

295
00:23:02,240 --> 00:23:06,640
How did you start getting that, that traction in attention?

296
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:17,440
Like where did those early, that early growth from twenty thirteen, fourteen, fifteen, how did you get that two x, two five, two point five x year over year?

297
00:23:18,960 --> 00:23:21,760
Honestly, part of it is I don't know.

298
00:23:22,480 --> 00:23:24,080
I like to think it was word of mouth.

299
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:28,240
I didn't have analytics in place, so I can only venture guesses at this point.

300
00:23:29,680 --> 00:23:31,280
I wasn't spending anything on ads.

301
00:23:31,680 --> 00:23:38,240
I think my first experiment from ads was 2016, 2017, somewhere in there.

302
00:23:38,640 --> 00:23:41,840
I did Instagram ads for like, I don't know, five thousand dollars or something.

303
00:23:41,920 --> 00:23:43,440
Like, I didn't throw a lot of money at it.

304
00:23:44,240 --> 00:23:48,000
It was, you know, a small percentage of my yearly downloads at that point.

305
00:23:48,640 --> 00:23:54,720
And so a lot of it was just word of mouth, luck that the ski community does tend to talk to each other.

306
00:23:55,040 --> 00:23:56,080
It's a group activity.

307
00:23:56,800 --> 00:24:01,440
You know, if you're out talking about your top speed at dinner, I'm going to be like, hey, how do you know how fast you went?

308
00:24:02,480 --> 00:24:14,000
So I feel like there's in that regard at least the core of the ski community, the people who go often, not just the people going out for holidays, they're going to talk, share things like that.

309
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:19,280
So I think that was definitely an advantage that helped my word of mouth efforts.

310
00:24:20,080 --> 00:24:22,480
Now I did try and I hope I was smart.

311
00:24:23,280 --> 00:24:33,360
I built like share cards inside a slope so you could post an image of your 3D rendered day basically to social media with your stats on there and stuff like that because people wanted to brag.

312
00:24:34,480 --> 00:24:37,120
So I tried to do some small things to encourage it.

313
00:24:38,160 --> 00:24:40,480
No real growth hacking or anything like that.

314
00:24:40,960 --> 00:24:45,520
And it was certainly not an ad dump like we see today to climb the trucks and do all that kind of stuff.

315
00:24:45,760 --> 00:24:45,840
Yeah.

316
00:24:46,080 --> 00:24:49,440
Honestly, it was just a lot of slow, slow building it.

317
00:24:49,920 --> 00:24:52,640
Um, and just being patient year over year with it.

318
00:24:53,120 --> 00:24:53,200
Yeah.

319
00:24:53,360 --> 00:24:59,360
I mean, that's, that's, I think, uh, people are always trying to figure out, like, this product market fit question or like, you know, whatever.

320
00:24:59,600 --> 00:25:05,040
But I think that's, that's probably one of the best signs is when things are growing and you don't know why.

321
00:25:05,920 --> 00:25:07,200
It's like, I don't know.

322
00:25:07,360 --> 00:25:08,080
I don't really do much.

323
00:25:08,320 --> 00:25:09,280
It gets bigger every year, right?

324
00:25:09,520 --> 00:25:14,400
That's, that's, the word of mouth is a very clear signal of product market fit and people that need it.

325
00:25:14,560 --> 00:25:24,320
And, and, and, I mean, that's why why I mean, we'll get further into how things have progressed from there, but that's kind of the first tell you see for a lot of apps or SaaS or whatever.

326
00:25:24,480 --> 00:25:26,000
It's like growth just kind of happens.

327
00:25:26,640 --> 00:25:35,120
Which is funny to think I found product market fit back then because Slopes 2.0, the one that launched in 2015, was an absolute steaming hole of horseshit.

328
00:25:36,080 --> 00:25:38,880
And I regret shipping it in many ways.

329
00:25:39,520 --> 00:25:44,800
I had some great ideas in there, but the fact that there was word of mouth around this app, I still surprised.

330
00:25:44,880 --> 00:25:45,360
Well, that's me.

331
00:25:45,440 --> 00:25:52,160
Now back then, I thought it was an amazing app, but Yeah, the funny thing with product market fit, it's not up to you to decide, right?

332
00:25:52,320 --> 00:25:53,040
It's up to the market.

333
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:57,760
And if the need is there and it solves the base case, it can be really bad, right?

334
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
And still, if people need it, they need it, right?

335
00:26:00,480 --> 00:26:00,560
Yeah.

336
00:26:01,280 --> 00:26:01,360
Yeah.

337
00:26:01,520 --> 00:26:02,640
A lot of apps live on that.

338
00:26:03,360 --> 00:26:03,440
Yeah.

339
00:26:04,800 --> 00:26:18,880
At what point or have you even tracked ASO and, you know, have you done some optimizations because I did find that you rank really high for real generic keywords like skiing, you know, today.

340
00:26:19,120 --> 00:26:22,640
So I haven't been tracking your ASO, you know, the last ten years obviously.

341
00:26:23,200 --> 00:26:29,520
But you rank really high for skiing, for snowboarding, for ski tracking, for snowboard tracking.

342
00:26:30,480 --> 00:26:36,960
Have you looked at your App Store analytics and kind of dug into how much of your traffic is coming from search?

343
00:26:37,200 --> 00:26:43,840
And that's really hard to know too, because it's like how many people are searching the actual name of the app versus searching keywords.

344
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:51,200
But I'm just curious, like, if ASO played into that growth in any measurable way that you were able to see.

345
00:26:52,720 --> 00:26:54,640
So I forget the date of it.

346
00:26:55,120 --> 00:26:56,880
I want to say probably around 2016.

347
00:26:57,680 --> 00:26:59,120
There might be a blog post about this.

348
00:26:59,440 --> 00:27:04,880
Please see my blog and hold that to the source of truth, not necessarily what I'm remembering right now.

349
00:27:05,440 --> 00:27:13,280
But I think it was around 2016, I did start to do, try and do all the right things with screenshots, ASO, all that kind of stuff.

350
00:27:13,920 --> 00:27:22,880
Fortunately, I know my way around Photoshop, so I could come up with some decent, you know, screenshots, stuff like that, experiment with different ideas in there.

351
00:27:23,360 --> 00:27:26,160
But that's, you know, the marketing side of things.

352
00:27:27,120 --> 00:27:32,320
It's a weird journey, because I remember in the beginning always hearing like, Oh, we're going to do all this development, but can't forget to market.

353
00:27:32,480 --> 00:27:34,240
And I'm like, Oh, yeah, sure, I'm going to market.

354
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:39,600
And then I try and pull like a Michael Simmons or something with a flexibits, you know, the big surprise announcement and everything.

355
00:27:39,920 --> 00:27:41,600
And that only works if you have a audience.

356
00:27:41,920 --> 00:27:43,840
And I didn't realize that key factor back then.

357
00:27:44,960 --> 00:27:47,120
And then I didn't really keep up with the marketing too much.

358
00:27:48,240 --> 00:27:57,920
But then I started to think, okay, I really need to pay attention to this and started to look at ASO and tried my very amateur attempts at doing a good job at that.

359
00:27:58,880 --> 00:28:06,800
I remember there was one blog post from AppFigures that was talking about like combining subtitles and titles, how you can play off those combinations of words.

360
00:28:07,200 --> 00:28:12,960
And that definitely helped rank for some of those more generic words like ski tracker and stuff like that.

361
00:28:13,120 --> 00:28:15,920
There wasn't just like keyword stuffing necessarily in the title.

362
00:28:16,240 --> 00:28:17,440
So I tried a bunch of that.

363
00:28:18,240 --> 00:28:24,240
So employee number one, Ella, who came in to help with some marketing growth, all that kind of stuff.

364
00:28:24,800 --> 00:28:33,120
She had definitely been giving a much more serious go at ASO stuff for probably the last two years or so, and that certainly helped a lot.

365
00:28:33,680 --> 00:28:50,080
So that's kind of if I tried ASO, yes, I'm sure I was an amateur, but I feel like one of the key things is, I don't know how true it is nowadays, but certainly back then, if you're at least trying, you're doing better than ninety percent of the people out there.

366
00:28:50,560 --> 00:28:58,000
Because most, especially indie developers, we vomit at the idea of marketing and ASO and screenshots and all that kind of stuff.

367
00:28:58,480 --> 00:29:00,800
So we kind of leave it to the last minute.

368
00:29:01,360 --> 00:29:07,200
So if you're actually spending a couple days a month trying this stuff, you're probably gonna have a leg up.

369
00:29:07,520 --> 00:29:09,040
Dan, you mentioned app figures.

370
00:29:09,200 --> 00:29:20,320
Like there's a lot of content out there to like for the for the for the developer who's engineering focused like trying to do growth, just copy, just find like the best example you can find and make it work for you.

371
00:29:20,800 --> 00:29:21,520
That will be good enough.

372
00:29:21,760 --> 00:29:22,720
You don't need to invent something.

373
00:29:22,960 --> 00:29:23,920
You don't need to be fancy.

374
00:29:24,240 --> 00:29:26,800
Start with that and then you can worry about your other problems down the road.

375
00:29:26,960 --> 00:29:32,800
But like having a little bit of an idea about growth and like it helps, you know, you should do it.

376
00:29:33,120 --> 00:29:39,200
And speaking of which, I'm super impressed that the first employee you hired was for marketing and growth.

377
00:29:39,280 --> 00:29:44,160
Because it's, I mean, it's just, you know, so typical of an indie mindset.

378
00:29:44,320 --> 00:29:49,440
Like I would have thought it would be designer or another developer to get push more features.

379
00:29:49,680 --> 00:29:54,800
But I think it was really smart that your first employee was for marketing and growth.

380
00:29:54,960 --> 00:30:10,320
So I'm curious kind of what your vision for that position was and then kind of how it's gone to have you doing kind of engineering and and pretty much everything else and then have someone like full time thinking about how to grow the product.

381
00:30:10,800 --> 00:30:13,280
So I'd like to say I was smart and had a plan for this.

382
00:30:13,600 --> 00:30:14,080
I didn't.

383
00:30:15,520 --> 00:30:20,720
So she was actually a customer who wrote in to me with a health kit bug way back in the day.

384
00:30:21,760 --> 00:30:29,680
And then we just kind of started chatting because my email signature for customer support still to this day says like, oh, can you leave a five star rating?

385
00:30:29,840 --> 00:30:31,520
They're like old independent developers.

386
00:30:32,800 --> 00:30:40,160
And she saw that, I believe, and was like, oh, wait, you're independent, you're a small shop doing this.

387
00:30:40,400 --> 00:30:41,600
And she snowboarded a lot.

388
00:30:41,760 --> 00:30:43,760
She was passionate about the space, so she was interested.

389
00:30:44,720 --> 00:30:48,400
And like marketing, ASO, all that kind of stuff was kind of her wheelhouse.

390
00:30:49,040 --> 00:31:05,760
So yeah, she just came in and proposed like a part time engagement thing to help me go through my like all the stuff I knew I should do by looking at the web and SAS, like, oh, I should be figuring out all these numbers, running all these analytics, coming up with customer segments, stuff like that.

391
00:31:06,080 --> 00:31:09,200
Like the things I knew I should do but just never made the time for.

392
00:31:10,240 --> 00:31:12,880
She came in and she's like, hey, I can do that stuff.

393
00:31:13,440 --> 00:31:14,240
I've done this before.

394
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:19,200
Can I get access to an anonymized version of your database and I'll figure this stuff out for you.

395
00:31:19,920 --> 00:31:25,200
So hired her part time to do that over one summer and it went really well, got a lot of great insights.

396
00:31:26,240 --> 00:31:28,400
And then just built on that from there.

397
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:37,120
And until what was it, May, almost a year, last May, that it was full time at that point.

398
00:31:37,600 --> 00:31:39,280
So it just kind of organically grew.

399
00:31:40,240 --> 00:31:47,440
But I did know hiring a developer or a designer, as tempting as it is, was the stupidest decision I could make.

400
00:31:49,040 --> 00:32:13,360
One of the things I've been lucky with is I can design, I can develop, I can do half decent UX, I can pretend I know marketing, I can balance my books and run zero and gusto and do all the HR stuff, like being able to do all this stuff has really enabled me to go far with slopes versus a lot of entrepreneurs that have to hire a developer and for that they need to seek funding.

401
00:32:13,760 --> 00:32:17,680
So the fact that I could do it myself, I think was definitely a bit of a superpower.

